  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened policy and legal framework for social protection, including reforms towards establishing a national social protection floor.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A large share of workers and their families in the Philippines remain without adequate social protection. According to the World Social Protection Report 2024&#x2013;26, only three in ten Filipinos are covered by at least one social protection benefit (excluding health). This gap is largely driven by the country&#x2019;s extensive informal economy and disproportionately affects overseas Filipino workers (OFWs), women engaged in unpaid care work, rural populations and other vulnerable groups.

In 2023, the proportion of total government spending on essential services (education, health and social protection) was 25.1 percent, of which only 4.3 percent was allocated to social protection.

PHL 133 addresses these challenges by strengthening the social protection system to make it more equitable, inclusive and gender-responsive (Outcome 7). The Country Programme Outcome (CPO) also integrates efforts to prevent and address violence and harassment in the world of work (Output 5.3) and supports the development of validated diagnoses and policy frameworks to extend labour protection to workers at high risk of exclusion due to their work arrangements (Output 6.3).
 
To achieve this, the ILO works with the Government and social partners to implement the Social Protection Floor recommendations; expand coverage to excluded groups, including informal and platform economy workers; assess the capacity of social protection institutions; and ensure that policies and programmes are rights-based and gender-responsive. This outcome supports the Labour and Employment Plan 2023&#x2013;2028 priority on social protection, advances the Social Protection Plan 2023&#x2013;2028, promotes the Social Protection Floor and addresses legal and policy gaps on violence and harassment at work in line with Convention No. 190. It also contributes to the UNSDCF priority on human capital development and priority on human capital development and resilience by improving access to quality services and strengthening institution.</narrative>
  </description>
